</math> is a quadratic function where <math alttext="f left parenthesis negative 20 right parenthesis equals 0"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext="f left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis equals 0"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. The graph of&nbsp;<math alttext="y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane has a vertex at&nbsp;<math alttext="left parenthesis r comma negative 64 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. What is the value of <math alttext="r"><mi>r</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["-12"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["-12"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="negative 12"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that function <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="f"><mi>f</mi></math> is a quadratic function where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="f left parenthesis negative 20 right parenthesis equals 0"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="f left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis equals 0"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. It follows that the graph of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane passes through&nbsp;the points <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis negative 20 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis negative 4 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. When the graph of a quadratic function contains two points <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis a comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis b comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, the <em>x</em>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is the average of&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="a"><mi>a</mi></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="b"><mi>b</mi></math>.&nbsp;Therefore, the <em>x</em>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction negative 20 plus left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="negative 12"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>. It's given that the graph of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane has a vertex at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis r comma negative 64 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. It follows that the value of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="r"><mi>r</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="negative 12"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"668f1863","external_id":"f78208f1-bb88-4092-9147-edda34ecda72","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"803d5eaa-008f-4024-aefb-6bf44612f6cf","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"P","domain":"Advanced Math","skill_cd":"P.C.","skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Function <math alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi>\n</math> is a quadratic function where <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 20 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. The graph of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane has a vertex at&nbsp;<math alttext=\"left parenthesis r comma negative 64 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. What is the value of <math alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":null,"keys":["-12"],"correct_answer":["-12"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi></math> is a quadratic function where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 20 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. It follows that the graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane passes through&nbsp;the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 20 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 4 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. When the graph of a quadratic function contains two points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis a comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis b comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, the <em>x</em>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is the average of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math>.&nbsp;Therefore, the <em>x</em>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction negative 20 plus left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>. It's given that the graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane has a vertex at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis r comma negative 64 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. It follows that the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017726,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"668f1863","skill_cd":"P.C.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","createDate":1755117017726,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"","external_id":"f78208f1-bb88-4092-9147-edda34ecda72","primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"803d5eaa-008f-4024-aefb-6bf44612f6cf","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Function <math alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi>\n</math> is a quadratic function where <math alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 20 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. The graph of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane has a vertex at&nbsp;<math alttext=\"left parenthesis r comma negative 64 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. What is the value of <math alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["-12"],"rationale":"<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"f\"><mi>f</mi></math> is a quadratic function where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 20 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"f left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis equals 0\"><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>. It follows that the graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane passes through&nbsp;the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 20 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 4 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. When the graph of a quadratic function contains two points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis a comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis b comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, the <em>x</em>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is the average of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"a\"><mi>a</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"b\"><mi>b</mi></math>.&nbsp;Therefore, the <em>x</em>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction negative 20 plus left parenthesis negative 4 right parenthesis Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>. It's given that the graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y equals f left parenthesis x right parenthesis\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></math> in the <em>xy</em>-plane has a vertex at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis r comma negative 64 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>)</mo></math>. It follows that the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"r\"><mi>r</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 12\"><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"f78208f1-bb88-4092-9147-edda34ecda72","correct_answer":["-12"]},"createDate":1755117017726,"updateDate":1755117017726}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017726, 1755117017726),
    ($SATQ$66bce0c1$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$11192-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables $SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference " xmlns="http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1"><p class="standalone_statement style:1 "><span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_004_a7958665.png" alt="The square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4 equals, x plus 3"></span></span></p></div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">What is the solution set of the equation above?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_000_3b8d547b.png\" alt=\"set consisting of negative 1\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_001_066d0a92.png\" alt=\"set consisting of 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_002_0886818f.png\" alt=\"set consisting of negative 1 and 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_003_86a8e172.png\" alt=\"set consisting of 0, negative 1, and 5\"></span></span></p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p><!--[if-->Choice B is correct. Subtracting 4 from both sides of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_005_a7958665.png" alt="the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, x plus 3"></span> isolates the radical expression on the left side of the equation as follows: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_006_c18d87ed.png" alt="the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, equals, x minus 1"></span>. Squaring both sides of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_007_c18d87ed.png" alt="the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, equals, x minus 1"></span> yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_008_d233f30a.png" alt="2 x plus 6, equals x squared, minus 2 x, plus 1"></span>. This equation can be rewritten as a quadratic equation in standard form: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_009_2262d2c9.png" alt="x squared, minus 4 x, minus 5, equals 0"></span>. One way to solve this quadratic equation is to factor the expression <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_010_be1dca14.png" alt="x squared, minus 4 x, minus 5"></span> by identifying two numbers with a sum of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_011_4d86fa3c.png" alt="negative 4"></span> and a product of <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_012_22df6a62.png" alt="negative 5"></span>. These numbers are <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_013_22df6a62.png" alt="negative 5"></span> and 1. So the quadratic equation can be factored as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_014_c1c5b9c2.png" alt="open parenthesis, x minus 5, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, x plus 1, close parenthesis, equals 0"></span>. It follows that 5 and <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_015_d3b569af.png" alt="negative 1"></span> are the solutions to the quadratic equation. However, the solutions must be verified by checking whether 5 and<span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_016_d3b569af.png" alt="negative 1"></span> satisfy the original equation, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_017_a7958665.png" alt="the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, x plus 3"></span>. When <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_018_921f97f2.png" alt="x equals negative 1"></span>, the original equation gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_019_3697dfc0.png" alt="the square root of 2 times negative 1, plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, negative 1 plus 3"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_020_728ef96a.png" alt="6 equals 2"></span>, which is false. Therefore, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_021_d3b569af.png" alt="negative 1"></span> does not satisfy the original equation. When <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_022_7fbddcb3.png" alt="x equals 5"></span>, the original equation gives <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_023_58b103e2.png" alt="the square root of 2 times 5, plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, 5 plus 3"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_024_3964d6f7.png" alt="8 equals 8"></span>, which is true. Therefore, <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_025_7fbddcb3.png" alt="x equals 5"></span> is the only solution to the original equation, and so the solution set is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_026_066d0a92.png" alt="5"></span>.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect because each of these sets contains at least one value that results in a false statement when substituted into the given equation. For instance, in choice D, when 0 is substituted for <span class="italic">x</span> into the given equation, the result is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_027_c1ab0a87.png" alt="the square root of 2 times 0, plus 6, plus 4, end root, equals, 0 plus 3"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_028_47762ef3.png" alt="the square root of 6, end root, plus 4, equals 3"></span>. This is not a true statement, so 0 is not a solution to the given equation.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 29, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"66bce0c1","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"11192-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"P","domain":"Advanced Math","skill_cd":"P.B.","skill_desc":"Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables ","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \" xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\"><p class=\"standalone_statement style:1 \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_004_a7958665.png\" alt=\"The square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4 equals, x plus 3\"></span></span></p></div>\n","stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">What is the solution set of the equation above?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_000_3b8d547b.png\" alt=\"set consisting of negative 1\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_001_066d0a92.png\" alt=\"set consisting of 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_002_0886818f.png\" alt=\"set consisting of negative 1 and 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_003_86a8e172.png\" alt=\"set consisting of 0, negative 1, and 5\"></span></span></p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p><!--[if-->Choice B is correct. Subtracting 4 from both sides of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_005_a7958665.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, x plus 3\"></span> isolates the radical expression on the left side of the equation as follows: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_006_c18d87ed.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, equals, x minus 1\"></span>. Squaring both sides of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_007_c18d87ed.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, equals, x minus 1\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_008_d233f30a.png\" alt=\"2 x plus 6, equals x squared, minus 2 x, plus 1\"></span>. This equation can be rewritten as a quadratic equation in standard form: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_009_2262d2c9.png\" alt=\"x squared, minus 4 x, minus 5, equals 0\"></span>. One way to solve this quadratic equation is to factor the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_010_be1dca14.png\" alt=\"x squared, minus 4 x, minus 5\"></span> by identifying two numbers with a sum of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_011_4d86fa3c.png\" alt=\"negative 4\"></span> and a product of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_012_22df6a62.png\" alt=\"negative 5\"></span>. These numbers are <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_013_22df6a62.png\" alt=\"negative 5\"></span> and 1. So the quadratic equation can be factored as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_014_c1c5b9c2.png\" alt=\"open parenthesis, x minus 5, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, x plus 1, close parenthesis, equals 0\"></span>. It follows that 5 and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_015_d3b569af.png\" alt=\"negative 1\"></span> are the solutions to the quadratic equation. However, the solutions must be verified by checking whether 5 and<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_016_d3b569af.png\" alt=\"negative 1\"></span> satisfy the original equation, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_017_a7958665.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, x plus 3\"></span>. When <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_018_921f97f2.png\" alt=\"x equals negative 1\"></span>, the original equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_019_3697dfc0.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 times negative 1, plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, negative 1 plus 3\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_020_728ef96a.png\" alt=\"6 equals 2\"></span>, which is false. Therefore, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_021_d3b569af.png\" alt=\"negative 1\"></span> does not satisfy the original equation. When <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_022_7fbddcb3.png\" alt=\"x equals 5\"></span>, the original equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_023_58b103e2.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 times 5, plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, 5 plus 3\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_024_3964d6f7.png\" alt=\"8 equals 8\"></span>, which is true. Therefore, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_025_7fbddcb3.png\" alt=\"x equals 5\"></span> is the only solution to the original equation, and so the solution set is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_026_066d0a92.png\" alt=\"5\"></span>.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect because each of these sets contains at least one value that results in a false statement when substituted into the given equation. For instance, in choice D, when 0 is substituted for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> into the given equation, the result is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_027_c1ab0a87.png\" alt=\"the square root of 2 times 0, plus 6, plus 4, end root, equals, 0 plus 3\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/66bce0c1/img_028_47762ef3.png\" alt=\"the square root of 6, end root, plus 4, equals 3\"></span>. This is not a true statement, so 0 is not a solution to the given equation.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":29,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959638,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"66bce0c1","skill_cd":"P.B.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables ","createDate":1691007959638,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"11192-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"5313ee7f-d48b-48ed-9bb4-49a315232ac1","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"11192-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference \" xmlns=\"http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1\"><p class=\"standalone_statement style:1 \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"The square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4 equals, x plus 3\"></span></span></p></div>\n","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">What is the solution set of the equation above?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"set consisting of negative 1\"></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"set consisting of 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"set consisting of negative 1 and 5\"></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"set consisting of 0, negative 1, and 5\"></span></span></p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"b","rationale":"<p><!--[if-->Choice B is correct. Subtracting 4 from both sides of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, x plus 3\"></span> isolates the radical expression on the left side of the equation as follows: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, equals, x minus 1\"></span>. Squaring both sides of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, equals, x minus 1\"></span> yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 x plus 6, equals x squared, minus 2 x, plus 1\"></span>. This equation can be rewritten as a quadratic equation in standard form: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x squared, minus 4 x, minus 5, equals 0\"></span>. One way to solve this quadratic equation is to factor the expression <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x squared, minus 4 x, minus 5\"></span> by identifying two numbers with a sum of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 4\"></span> and a product of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 5\"></span>. These numbers are <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"negative 5\"></span> and 1. So the quadratic equation can be factored as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"open parenthesis, x minus 5, close parenthesis, times, open parenthesis, x plus 1, close parenthesis, equals 0\"></span>. It follows that 5 and <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAABcAAAAYCAYAAAARfGZ1AAAACXBIWXMAAA7EAAAOxAGVKw4bAAAABGJhU0UAAAAMyZLetQAAAFhJREFUeNpjYKAMtADxNiB+DsT/oXyqgS9AfAyIp9LCcCYkNtUNZyDWcBEiMBu5hv8nAoeQa7gLEViMXMNpGuYjy/AQJAwyfA0SnyquxYVHwSgYBaOAJgAAQ7AqWC3zu8cAAAAASUVORK5CYII=\" alt=\"negative 1\"></span> are the solutions to the quadratic equation. However, the solutions must be verified by checking whether 5 and<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAABcAAAAYCAYAAAARfGZ1AAAACXBIWXMAAA7EAAAOxAGVKw4bAAAABGJhU0UAAAAMyZLetQAAAFhJREFUeNpjYKAMtADxNiB+DsT/oXyqgS9AfAyIp9LCcCYkNtUNZyDWcBEiMBu5hv8nAoeQa7gLEViMXMNpGuYjy/AQJAwyfA0SnyquxYVHwSgYBaOAJgAAQ7AqWC3zu8cAAAAASUVORK5CYII=\" alt=\"negative 1\"></span> satisfy the original equation, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 x plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, x plus 3\"></span>. When <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals negative 1\"></span>, the original equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 times negative 1, plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, negative 1 plus 3\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"6 equals 2\"></span>, which is false. Therefore, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAABcAAAAYCAYAAAARfGZ1AAAACXBIWXMAAA7EAAAOxAGVKw4bAAAABGJhU0UAAAAMyZLetQAAAFhJREFUeNpjYKAMtADxNiB+DsT/oXyqgS9AfAyIp9LCcCYkNtUNZyDWcBEiMBu5hv8nAoeQa7gLEViMXMNpGuYjy/AQJAwyfA0SnyquxYVHwSgYBaOAJgAAQ7AqWC3zu8cAAAAASUVORK5CYII=\" alt=\"negative 1\"></span> does not satisfy the original equation. When <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 5\"></span>, the original equation gives <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 times 5, plus 6, end root, plus 4, equals, 5 plus 3\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"8 equals 8\"></span>, which is true. Therefore, <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 5\"></span> is the only solution to the original equation, and so the solution set is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"5\"></span>.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect because each of these sets contains at least one value that results in a false statement when substituted into the given equation. For instance, in choice D, when 0 is substituted for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> into the given equation, the result is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 2 times 0, plus 6, plus 4, end root, equals, 0 plus 3\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of 6, end root, plus 4, equals 3\"></span>. This is not a true statement, so 0 is not a solution to the given equation.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959638,"updateDate":1691007959638}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959638, 1691007959638),
    ($SATQ$67e866b5$SATQ$, $SATQ$9c3d170a-52ba-4266-b573-bfa73fbbe581$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5a7f67dc-227a-4931-8dac-a9300b25e743$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Equivalent expressions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Which expression is equivalent to <math alttext="9 x squared plus 7 x squared plus 9 x"><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi></math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"276cb650-6bc1-4c8f-9097-026bbedcffc1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"63 x Superscript 4 plus 9 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>63</mn>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"0236ed34-2401-4025-8a85-d41ee60d9dc2","content":"<p><math alttext=\"9 x squared plus 16 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>16</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"b300e5b0-4ca2-4887-9683-a5d0e95a814a","content":"<p><math alttext=\"25 x Superscript 5\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>25</mn>\n\t<msup>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t</msup>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"362d80b5-7cd1-43e2-8d29-19c0cb9eb27d","content":"<p><math alttext=\"16 x squared plus 9 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>16</mn>\n\t\t<msup>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t\t</msup>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>+</mo>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["362d80b5-7cd1-43e2-8d29-19c0cb9eb27d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. In the given expression, the first two terms,&nbsp;<math alttext="9 x squared"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> and <math alttext="7 x squared"><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, are like terms. Combining these like terms yields <math alttext="9 x squared plus 7 x squared"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="16 x squared"><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It follows that the expression&nbsp;<math alttext="9 x squared plus 7 x squared plus 9 x"><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi></math> is equivalent to <math alttext="16 x squared plus 9 x"><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi></math>.</p>
